# SOC Ticketing Application

## Overview

Developed a Python Flask-based incident management application to receive security alerts from Splunk and maintain incident tickets.

## Core Components

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Flask | Web application and webhook endpoint |
| SQLite | Persistent ticket storage |
| JSON | Alert payload processing |
| HTTP POST | Splunk integration |
| HTML/CSS/JavaScript | Dashboard interface |

## Webhook Endpoint

`POST /webhook`

The endpoint receives JSON alert information from Splunk and processes the incident.

## Example Payload

```json
{
  "search_name": "SSH Brute Force",
  "result": {
    "incident": "Possible SSH Brute Force Attack",
    "src_ip": "192.168.113.131",
    "dest_ip": "192.168.113.136",
    "username": "rahul",
    "severity": "High",
    "failed_attempts": 10
  }
}
```

## Ticket Management

- Generate unique SOC ticket IDs.
- Store incident information in SQLite.
- Track severity and occurrence counts.
- Prevent duplicate ticket creation according to the application's matching logic.
- Manage Open, In Progress and Resolved statuses.

## Dashboard Features

- Ticket overview.
- Search by ticket, alert, IP or username.
- Severity and status filters.
- Incident detail view.
- Ticket status updates.

## Example Webhook Response

```json
{
  "duplicate": false,
  "message": "Ticket created successfully",
  "ticket_id": "SOC-0F7DD0D6"
}
```

The example illustrates a successful ticket creation response from the lab test.

## Security Considerations

- Validate webhook input.
- Restrict access to the endpoint.
- Protect the SQLite database.
- Avoid exposing sensitive event information.
- Use secure deployment settings in production.
